// Bun Snapshot v1, https://bun.sh/docs/test/snapshots

exports[`brain --help and every core command's --help print the snapshotted output and exit code 1`] = `
{
  "--help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"Odysseus's voyage notebook

Usage: brain <command> [args] [flags]

Commands:
  accept-mtime  Baseline current file mtimes so silent-edit checks stop flagging them
  add           Quick-add content to the brain
  apify         Discover, inspect and run Apify Actors under the governance policy
  archive       Archive a document (set status: archived)
  audit         Run staleness and quality audit
  briefing      Emit a mechanical daily briefing (deadlines, reviews, silent edits)
  compute       External compute: plan, run, inspect, cancel and collect jobs on a configured provider
  config        Validate config / read effective taxonomy and config values
  context       Assemble a context block for agent consumption
  doctor        Run the health check battery (add --fix to auto-repair)
  eval          Score a retrieval query set against this brain's index
  geo           Geocode, route, find POIs, measure tracks and export static maps
  graph         Inspect and rebuild the derived wiki-link graph
  hygiene       Reconcile, read and triage the content-hygiene log
  import        Mechanical frontmatter stamping for imported markdown
  index         Update the search index (incremental by default)
  init          Preflight check (--check) or non-interactive default setup (--default)
  list          List/browse documents
  maintain      Run routine maintenance: registry tables, incremental index, vector compaction, audit snapshot, stats history, tag report, git packing, scratch prune
  mcp           Start the stdio MCP server
  module        List and lint brain-kit modules
  okf           Export or validate an Open Knowledge Format v0.1 bundle
  policy        Validate the read-only governance policy without loading brain configuration
  process       Assimilate a note into proper brain content
  queue         Queue intake on a UI server without filing content
  read          Read and print a document from the brain
  read-many     Read several documents or sections under one size cap
  registry      Regenerate _index.md registry tables from the children's frontmatter
  render        Render a document to PDF, PNG, or standalone HTML
  schedule      Manage scheduled tasks on a UI host (no local runner)
  scratch       Clean or prune the brain's scratch area
  search        Hybrid search across the knowledge base
  setup         Idempotent setup: git hooks, skills sync, bin links
  skills        Distribute (sync) or lint the skill suite
  stats         Show corpus statistics
  storage       Bounded storage inventory and non-destructive Git packing
  sync          Sync the brain with its git remote (run the whole sync, or one verb of it)
  tags          Report tag variants, redundant tags and vocabulary drift (read-only)
  validate      Run config, frontmatter, link, and index-drift validation

Global flags:
  --json      Force JSON output
  --human     Force human-readable output
  --help      Show help (append to a command for its usage)
  --version   Show the installed @schlessera/brain version
"
,
  },
  "accept-mtime --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain accept-mtime [path] — baseline current file mtimes as non-suspicious

Stops silent-edit checks from flagging deliberate mechanical migrations
(all documents, or a single path).
"
,
  },
  "add --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain add "<content>" — quick-capture content into the brain

  --type <type>           Explicit document type
  --title <title>         Explicit title
  --tags <a,b,c>          Comma-separated tags
  --smart                 Delegate to the coding agent (search, classify, route)
"
,
  },
  "apify --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain apify <operation> [--session <name>] [--json]

  discover <words...> [--limit N]                  Search the Actor catalog
  inspect <candidate>                              Read one Actor's build, schema and price
  prepare <candidate> --input <json> --max-charge-usd <usd>
                                                   Prepare one run; starts nothing
  run <proposal>                                   Run a proposal once
  status <run>                                     A run's state
  results <run> [--cursor C] [--limit N]           One page of results
  abort <run>                                      Ask Apify to stop a run
  availability [actor-id]                          Module/workflow readiness; no paid work
  approvals                                        Remembered Actor approvals
  revoke <actor-id>                                 Revoke remembered Actor approval
  account [confirm <account-id>]                   The bound account; confirm a changed one

The same operations as the brain_apify_* MCP tools, under the governance
policy's apify block. Needs APIFY_TOKEN and BRAIN_APIFY_LEDGER (the shared
ledger file). Nothing is ever asked on stdin: a run needs a remembered Actor
approval, else it refuses as approval_required. Every operation
prints { ok, operation, data } or { ok: false, operation, error: { code,
reason, message } } and exits 0 or 1.
"
,
  },
  "archive --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain archive <path> — set status: archived and a primary or unset relevance to historical, move projects/active → projects/archive, reindex

  --dry-run               Show what would happen without changing anything
"
,
  },
  "audit --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain audit — staleness and quality audit

  --fix                   Report deterministic repair availability and suggestions
                          (no provider call; applies nothing)

--json envelope: { "issues": AuditIssue[], "errors", "warnings", "infos",
"mustFix", "informational" } (markdown documents only — assets excluded).
Each count is findings, not markers: a document's TODO markers are one
finding, and so are its VERIFY markers. Must-fix is errors plus warnings;
informational is infos.
"
,
  },
  "briefing --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain briefing — mechanical daily status (no LLM)

  --limit <n>             Max recently-active docs (default: 15)
  --limit-reviews <n>     Overdue reviews to list before "… and N more" (default: 5)
"
,
  },
  "compute --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain compute health [--provider ID] [--json]
brain compute plan --provider ID [run options] -- COMMAND [ARGS...]
brain compute run --provider ID [run options] [--detach] -- COMMAND [ARGS...]
brain compute status JOB [--json]
brain compute cancel JOB [--json]
brain compute collect JOB [--json]
brain compute abandon JOB --confirm [--json]
brain compute reconcile [--json]

Run options:
  --provider ID          configured instance (compute.providers key); required, no default
  --cwd PATH             working directory relative to the staged source root
  --env NAME=VALUE       non-secret sandbox variable (repeatable); credential names are refused
  --source PATH          repository-relative file to upload (repeatable); nothing else is uploaded
  --artifact PATH[:MAX]  file to fetch back, with an optional byte cap (repeatable; default 1 MiB)
  --lease SECONDS        lease length; default and maximum is the instance's lease.maxSeconds
  --retain               keep the sandbox's data on release; only where the instance sets retainOnRelease
  --detach               run only: return once the command has started

Nothing runs remotely unless --provider names an instance. Job ids belong to
the operating-system user that started them through the CLI. Exit 1 is a usage
error or a refusal (nothing allocated); exit 2 a job whose cleanup was not
confirmed (run \`brain compute reconcile\`); exit 3 a job that ended other
than succeeded. A second Ctrl-C leaves an attached run at once (exit 130).
"
,
  },
  "config --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain config <check|get|media>

  check                Validate the config and print the effective taxonomy summary
  get <dotted.path>    Print a resolved config value as JSON (used by skills)
  media <add-track|remove-track|add-ignore|remove-ignore> <pattern>
      [--dry-run | --expect-sha <sha256> [--expect-approval <digest>]]
                       Change one media.track or media.ignore entry by a minimal
                       splice; the config is edited as syntax and never evaluated.
                       --dry-run previews: { "status": "preview", "op", "pattern",
                       "scope": "exact"|"pattern", "config": { "path", "sha256" },
                       "configDiff", "matches": { "pattern", "anyFolder", "count",
                       "bytes", "changed", "ignoredStill", "sensitive", "largest",
                       "digest" }, "approvalDigest", "refusals" }. Apply needs the
                       preview's config sha256, and with --expect-approval its
                       approvalDigest: { "status": "applied", "op", "pattern",
                       "config": { "path", "before", "after" }, "configDiff" }.
                       Otherwise { "status": "refused"|"stale", "op", "pattern",
                       "reason", "refusals" } and nothing is written; reasons are
                       stale, stale-approval, already-listed, not-listed,
                       not-expressible, invalid-pattern, config-not-literal and
                       config-invalid.
"
,
  },
  "context --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain context <query> — assemble a token-limited context block

  --max-tokens <n>        Token budget (default: 4000)
  --include <path>        Only draw on this path; repeatable. An exact file
                          path, or a folder ending in "/" for everything under it
  --exclude <path>        Never draw on this path; repeatable. Wins over --include

Output: assembled markdown context (plain text, never JSON). With --include or
--exclude, a "### Coverage" note follows it, outside the budget.
"
,
  },
  "doctor --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain doctor — health check battery

  --fix                   Apply the auto-fixable checks (hooks, symlinks, index,
                          legacy index files, deps, mcp, scratch, cache-merge),
                          then re-run and report.

--json: { "checks": [{ "id", "status": "pass"|"warn"|"fail", "detail", "fix"? }] }
"
,
  },
  "eval --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain eval — score a retrieval query set against this brain's index

  --set <file>            JSONL query set (default: evals/retrieval.jsonl)
  --mode <mode>           fts|vector|hybrid|all (default: hybrid)
  --rerank <mode>         none|heuristic|jev (default: the search default);
                          meta.reranker names the judgment reranker, if any
  --k <list>              hit@k cutoffs, comma-separated (default: 1,3,10)
  --out <file>            Also write the JSON result to <file>, inside the brain
  --strict                Refuse (exit 2) when an indexed document quotes the set's queries
  --now <ISO date>        The moment the run measures from, when the set's header
                          does not pin one (default: the wall clock)
  --context               Also run each query through \`brain context\` at each budget
                          and report whether the answer is in it (the "context" block)
  --budgets <list>        Context budgets in tokens, comma-separated
                          (default: 1000,4000,8000; needs --context)
  --lint                  Validate the set and report title leakage in its
                          "paraphrase" queries, without scoring or opening the
                          index: exit 0 with findings, 2 on a malformed set

Relative paths are relative to the brain root.

Against a stored run (one written with --out):
  --baseline <file>       Compare per query on hit@k; gate on hit@1
  --max-net-loss <n>      Fail when lost − gained on hit@1 reaches n (default: 2)
  --must-pass <classes>   Fail when any query in these classes (comma-separated) is lost
  --allow-set-change      Compare the queries both runs share when the set changed
  --redact                Leave query text and every path out of the output, --out
                          and a refusal's details; an error about a path you passed
                          (--set, --baseline, --out) still repeats it

Exit codes: 0 scored (and the gate passed) · 1 the baseline gate failed · 3 not
comparable with the baseline (a different set, mode, embedding model or k) ·
2 refused or misused: a usage error or malformed set (unlike other brain
commands, so 1 always means a regression), or a validity gate failed (missing
or empty set, an expected or stale path not on disk or not indexed, a
selector that selects nothing, the index older than the markdown, a requested
search lane degraded; with --strict, an indexed document that contains the
set's queries).

--json envelope: { "schema_version", "meta", "rows", "per_query", "warnings" },
plus "context": { "budgets", "rows", "per_query" } with --context
(--lint: { "schema_version", "meta": { "set", "set_sha256", "queries" }, "warnings" })
Set format and how to read the numbers: docs/evaluating-search.md
"
,
  },
  "geo --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain geo <geocode|route|poi|track|map> — shared geo operations

Coordinates are latitude,longitude in every CLI argument; bounds are
west,south,east,north. Decimal numbers are finite and source coordinates stay intact.

  geocode <query>                 Forward lookup; ambiguity remains visible.
  geocode --reverse <lat,lon>      Reverse lookup; address accuracy stays unknown.
  route <lat,lon>... --mode <car|foot|bike>
                                 Ordered stops through a verified prepared dataset.
  poi --near <lat,lon> | --along <file.gpx>
      --radius-m <metres> --tag <key[=value]>...
                                 Exact AND tags; a bare key means presence.
  track <file.gpx> [--near <lat,lon>] [--compare <file.gpx>]
      [--tolerance-m <metres>] [--sample-spacing-m <metres>]
                                 Recovered usable-section summary. Near/compare
                                 require tolerance; spacing requires compare.
  map [file.gpx...] [--pin <lat,lon,label>]...
      [--point <lat,lon>]... [--mode <car|foot|bike>]
      [--bbox <west,south,east,north>] [--title <text>] [--width <pixels>]
      [--no-background] --out <file.png>
                                 Resolve optional routing/background first, then
                                 rasterize locally. Width 320–2048, default 1024.

Files and output must remain inside the brain, including through symlinks.
Map needs an initialized brain; a chosen --out replaces an existing regular file.
Geo config sets endpoints, identifying User-Agent, eligibility and disposable cache.
Every new service is off by default. No implicit public request or route substitute.

--json emits one document with operation and the full shared result. Track includes
omissions/nullable proximity; map includes artifact {path,format,bytes} or null,
complete source/text/legend and kind/reason, with no embedded PNG/SVG bytes.
--human prints source, unknowns, partial status and applicable attribution.
Exit 0: valid result/fallback; 1: usage/input; 2: service/storage/renderer failure.
"
,
  },
  "graph --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain graph <compute|export|stats>

  compute [--root <path>]     Rebuild the derived graph tables from the index
  export --mode <mode> ...    Dump one graph view
  stats                       Node/edge/component counts, communities, root

export modes:
  clusters      Whole vault with communities and precomputed layout
                --community <n>   Restrict to one community
                --no-isolates     Drop notes with no links at all
  local         Neighbourhood around one note
                --center <path>   Required
                --depth <1-3>     Default 1
                --direction <in|out|both>   Default both
  discovery     Rings outward from the root
                --root <path>     Default: the precomputed root
                --direction <in|out|both>   Default out
                --depth <1-8>     Default 8
  maintenance   Orphans, unreachable notes, broken links, stale notes
                --stale-days <n>  Default 180

--json: the mode's payload. Graph tables are rebuilt by every index run; they
stay empty until the first one on schema v8.
"
,
  },
  "hygiene --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain hygiene <reconcile|propagation|list|next|dismiss|snooze|keep-copy|unkeep|media|resolve|undo|check> — the content-hygiene log under context/hygiene/

  propagation [--dry-run]
      Inspect or run diff-assisted freshness review after persistent opt-in
      (hygiene.propagation.enabled). Reports settings, typed judgments and
      threshold sensitivity. Dry-run may call Jev but writes no Markdown.
      Only a matching separate review receipt clears propagation; document
      content and updated dates are untouched. Unknowns remain for the owner.
      Ordinary reconcile/next run this prepass automatically when enabled;
      inspection-only next --dry-run never initiates provider calls.

  reconcile [--extra <file.json>] [--fixed <file.json>] [--dry-run]
      Refresh the index, detect issues (brain audit, brain validate's corpus
      checks, silent edits, index table lag, and --extra candidates), join
      reports of the same problem into one finding with a stable ID, and
      apply the open/snoozed/dismissed/kept/resolved state machine to the
      log, writing its files only where they change. Media awaiting a decision
      (the uncommitted MEDIA and LARGE files sync assesses, plus the scratch
      files a conversation links) are findings of category "media"; a kept
      entry (kept.md) holds while its permanent copy exists and is tracked. --extra is a JSON array of
      { "category", "path", "evidence", "message" } with optional "severity"
      (error|warning|info) and "urgency"; --fixed is a JSON array of
      { "path", "fix" }, the auto-fixes to record in last-run.md. When a check
      cannot run (a module's check throws, validation or fact-drift cannot read
      its input), it is named in "failedChecks" and no entry is resolved
      unless it was detected again. --dry-run writes no log file; the
      index is still refreshed.
      --json envelope: { "opened", "reopened", "resolved", "stillOpen", "snoozed", "dismissed",
      "invalidated", "invalidations", "changedFiles", "detected": [{ "id", "category", "path",
      "message", "severity", "urgency", "sources", "fingerprint" }], "autoFixed", "failedChecks",
      "kept" }

  next [--extra <file.json>] [--finding <id> --dry-run] [--defer <id>=<fp>,...]
      Reconcile and select one eligible canonical finding by severity, known
      urgency, age, then identity. Informational findings are counted but not
      shown, except media, which is always shown. The finding's handlers
      describe every available repair or manual choice; handler remains
      "manual" for compatibility. --defer skips findings left pending this
      review pass while their fingerprint is still <fp>. Returns
      { "finding", "counts", "media": { "awaitingReview", "setAside", "kept",
      "links" } }, the same media counts brain sync reports; invalid configuration returns
      { "blocker": { "kind": "configuration", "message", "path", "line", "column" } }
      with exit 1 before any indexing or hygiene writes.
      --finding <id> --dry-run reads that detected open finding without
      Markdown writes, preserving the log for counts; the index is refreshed.

  list [--state open|snoozed|dismissed|resolved|kept]
      The log's entries.
      --json envelope: { "entries": [{ "id", "state", "path", "issue", "firstSeen",
      "lastSeen", "until", "dueAt", "resolvedBy", "resolvedOn", "sources", "severity",
      "fingerprint", "disposition", "invalidation", "copy", "copySha256", "keptOn" }] }

  dismiss <id> --expect-fingerprint <fp> [--reason <text>] [--extra <file.json>]
  snooze <id> --until <date|date-time> --expect-fingerprint <fp> [--reason <text>] [--extra <file.json>]
      Reconcile, then record the disposition in dismissed.md or snoozed.md
      with the fingerprint it applies to. A dismissed finding stays out of
      review until its evidence changes; a snoozed one until --until arrives
      (an ISO date, or a date-time with a zone) or its evidence changes. When
      the finding is not detected now, or its fingerprint is not <fp>, it is
      refused: exit 1, nothing written. A finding the skill reported through
      reconcile --extra is detected only with the same --extra file.
      --json envelope: { "status": "dismissed"|"snoozed", "id", "fingerprint", "until",
      "reason", "changedFiles" }; refused: { "status": "refused", "reason":
      "not-detected"|"stale-fingerprint", "id", "expectedFingerprint", "currentFingerprint" }

  keep-copy <id> --expect-fingerprint <fp> --copy <path> --copy-sha256 <sha256> --journal <id> [--reason <text>]
      Reconcile, then record a media finding as kept (kept.md): its permanent
      copy at <path> was made and verified by Keep a permanent copy. Refused,
      with nothing written, unless the copy is a contained regular file outside
      .brain/ and workspaces/, media.track keeps it, and it holds <sha256>.
      --json envelope: as dismiss, with "status": "kept", "copy", "copySha256"
      and "journal" appended; refused reasons add "not-media", "copy-missing",
      "copy-untracked" and "copy-changed".

  unkeep <id> --journal <id>
      Reconcile, then remove the kept entry that keep-copy recorded for <id>
      under that journal ID: Undo of Keep a permanent copy, after the copy and
      its config are restored. The original is open again, without an
      invalidation receipt. Refused, with nothing written, when no such kept
      entry exists.
      --json envelope: { "status": "unkept", "id", "journal", "changedFiles" };
      refused: { "status": "refused", "reason": "not-kept", "id", "journal" }
  media <delete|move|persist|keep-copy> <id> --expect-fingerprint <fp> [--to <path>]
      [--pattern <glob> [--ignore-adjust <entry>]]
      [--dry-run | --expect-premise <digest> --journal <id> [--expect-approval <digest>]]
      A media finding's effect (#2781). --dry-run plans it and writes nothing:
      { "status": "preview", "id", "action", "fingerprint", "source", "destination",
      "links", "references", "rewrites", "blockers", "configDiff", "scope",
      "pattern", "ignoreAdjust", "matches", "approvalDigest", "captureBytes",
      "copy", "resultClass", "files", "premiseDigest", "refusals" }. move and
      keep-copy need --to. persist and keep-copy track the file by its exact
      path; --pattern tracks it by a broader media.track rule that matches it,
      and --ignore-adjust removes the one broader media.ignore entry that would
      still win. A broader change previews its current matches ("matches") and
      an approvalDigest that apply needs back as --expect-approval; a change
      since is "stale-approval". Apply needs the preview's premiseDigest and the
      caller's journal ID: the caller retains every listed pre-image first and
      compensates from it; this command never does. Apply returns { "status":
      "applied"|"stale"|"refused"|"interrupted", "id", "action", "journal",
      "reason", "refusals", "files": [{ ..., "state" }], "copySha256" }.
      keep-copy writes the copy and its config only; brain hygiene keep-copy
      records the kept entry after the caller has verified it.

  resolve <id> --handler link-suggested|link-note|link-text|required-field
      --input <json> --expect-fingerprint <fp> [--dry-run | --expect-preview <token>]
      Preview exact bytes without writes, then apply that preview's bounded
      effect after premise revalidation. A failed post-check leaves the finding
      open and returns an undoToken. No automatic Undo.
  undo <undoToken> [--dry-run]
      Preview or confirm the inverse effect. Refuses if the file changed.
  check <id>
      Check one finding. Still detected/failed checks write nothing; only a
      successful check records resolved-by: check. Use configuration-blocker
      to recheck invalid brain.config without writing anything.

"
,
  },
  "import --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain import --stamp <dir> — mechanical frontmatter stamping for imports

Stamps every un-frontmattered *.md under <dir> with: type=<inbox type>,
status=draft, title from the first H1 (else the filename), created/updated from
the file mtime. Files that already have frontmatter are skipped. JSON report.
"
,
  },
  "index --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain index — update the search index (incremental by default)

  --force                 Full rebuild instead of incremental update
  --embeddings            Generate vector embeddings (requires an API key)
  --incremental           Accepted as a no-op (backward compat)
  --on-commit             Run as the post-commit hook does: adds the
                          embeddings pass only when the config sets
                          hooks.embedOnCommit and a provider is configured
  --forget-cache <path>   Discard the cached contexts or description of one
                          document or asset, so the next --embeddings run
                          generates them again. Runs no index pass.
  --compact               Rebuild the vector table from its live rows and
                          VACUUM, reclaiming the slots deleted vectors leave.
                          No provider call. Runs no index pass.

--json: IndexStats object; with --forget-cache, { path, forgotten }; with
--compact, { compacted, before, after }.
"
,
  },
  "init --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain init — preflight check / non-interactive default setup

  --check                 Emit a preflight JSON report (no changes)
  --default               Write a minimal brain.config + core dirs, then index+validate

The conversational interview lives in the /brain-init skill; this command is its
mechanical backend and the keyless CI smoke path.
"
,
  },
  "list --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain list — list/browse documents

  --type <type>           Filter by document type
  --tag <tag>             Filter by tag
  --status <status>       Filter by status (active|archived|draft)
  --relevance <level>     Filter by relevance
  --limit <n>             Max results, 1-100 (default: 20)
  --path-prefix <path>    Only documents at or below this folder or file (whole segments)
  --overview              Add summary, updated, deadline, indexed_at, indexed_body_bytes
  --cursor <cursor>       Continue a paged listing from its next_cursor

--json: SearchResult[]; with --path-prefix, --overview or --cursor,
        { documents, next_cursor, total, truncated?, warnings } (one bounded page)
"
,
  },
  "maintain --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain maintain — routine maintenance (cron-friendly)

Runs, in order: the _index.md registry tables (the same step as
\`brain registry\`, which writes only generated regions whose table changed),
an incremental index (+embeddings when a key is configured), a
vector-table compaction when fewer than half its slots are live (the same step
as \`brain index --compact\`), an audit snapshot (the counts \`brain audit\`
reports, module hygiene checks included, with its must-fix and informational
totals), a stats snapshot (the day's
\`brain stats\` figures kept in .stats-history.jsonl, one per day — the same
step as \`brain stats --record\`), a tag report (counts only;
see \`brain tags\`), a git packing pass when the brain is a git work tree (git's
non-destructive loose-objects, incremental-repack and pack-refs tasks), then a
prune of the scratch area (files older than 7 days, then the oldest until
under 1 GB). Exits 2 if any step failed; the tag report never fails the run.
A failed run's last stderr line names each failed step and the start of its
result, so a caller that keeps only the end of stderr still sees which step
failed.
Module cron jobs are separate (advisory manifest entries consumed by the
container entrypoint).

  --no-git                Skip the git packing step

The hosting container runs this daily. A brain with no chat server has no
other periodic pass over the scratch area, so schedule this command (cron) or
scratch is pruned only when something writes into it.
"
,
  },
  "mcp --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain mcp — start the stdio MCP server

This command communicates over stdin/stdout and is intended for MCP clients.
"
,
  },
  "module --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain module <list|lint|enable|disable|settings>

  list          Enabled modules + available @schlessera/brain-module-* packages
  lint <name>   Validate an enabled module: manifest, skills, command/type
                collisions, MCP tools, and configSchema against the user's config block
  enable <name> Reactivate a configured module and synchronize owned context
  disable <name> Park workflows while retaining validated config and content
  settings <name> [--set key=value] [--revision REV] --json
                Read or save validated per-module settings
    --migrate [--preview]  Preview/apply a declared lossless content migration

Legacy mixed generated instruction sections require explicit migration first.
See docs/modules.md (instruction migration).
"
,
  },
  "okf --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain okf — export and validate Open Knowledge Format v0.1 bundles

  brain okf export [--out <dir>] [--include <dir>]... [--exclude <dir>]... [--no-assets]
  brain okf check [<dir>]

Export defaults to okf-dist/ and mirrors the indexed content scope. Use
--exclude repeatedly before sharing a bundle to remove private domains.
The report always lists the included top-level directories for review.

The output directory must be inside the brain root and excluded from indexing.
check defaults to okf-dist/ and exits 1 when the bundle has conformance errors.

--json export envelope: OkfExportReport
--json check envelope:  { directory, ok, filesChecked, errors, warnings, issues }
"
,
  },
  "policy --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain policy check [--json]

Reads context/policies/governance.md. Unknown keys and non-tier-B releases refuse.
Missing policy uses built-in defaults. No file is changed and no brain configuration executes.
"
,
  },
  "process --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain process <path> — assimilate a note into proper brain content

  --keep-note             Keep the original note after processing
  --all                   Batch-process all unprocessed inbox notes

Determines merge/promote/split/keep via a completion provider (Tier-2: needs an
API key). The CLI reports the decision; a skill applies the operations.
"
,
  },
  "queue --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"  brain queue add --server ORIGIN --key KEY [--credential-file FILE] [--title TITLE] [--text TEXT] [--url URL] [--json]
"
,
  },
  "read --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain read <path> — print a document (frontmatter included)

  --section <heading>     Print one section: its heading to the next heading
                          of the same or higher level
  --max-tokens <n>        When the output would be larger, print the
                          frontmatter and an outline of headings instead.
                          A threshold, not a cap: a large frontmatter or
                          very many headings make the outline larger

Headings are matched on their visible text, ignoring case; when two match,
the first is used. With neither flag the whole file is printed, however long
it is.
"
,
  },
  "read-many --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain read-many <path>... — read several documents under one size cap

  --input <file.json>     Read the whole request from a JSON file instead:
                          { "requests": [{ "path", "section"?, "offset"?,
                          "column"?, "expected_sha256"? }], "max_total_chars"?,
                          "max_item_chars"? } — the brain_read_many input
  --stdin                 The same JSON, from standard input
  --max-total-chars <n>   Cap on the whole JSON result, 2000-40000
                          (default 24000)
  --max-item-chars <n>    Cap on one item's JSON, 512 to the total cap
                          (default 8000)

Up to 20 requests. Each item is full, outline, partial (with next
offset/column to continue), duplicate or error; see brain_read_many.

--json: { items, truncated, max_total_chars, max_item_chars, warnings }
"
,
  },
  "registry --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain registry — regenerate _index.md registry tables from the children's frontmatter

An _index.md opts in with a \`registry:\` block in its frontmatter (columns,
optional where / sort / split). The table is written between
<!-- brain:generated:registry --> markers; the prose around it is kept, and
\`updated\` is bumped only on a file whose table changed.

  --check                 Write nothing; list out-of-date indexes (exit 1 if any)

--json: { indexes, written, stale, invalid: [{ path, error }] }
"
,
  },
  "render --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain render <path|-> — render a document to PDF, PNG, or standalone HTML

The input path must be inside the brain; use - for stdin.

  --format <fmt>        pdf (default), png, or html
  --out <path>          Output path, inside the brain. Default: the input path
                        with the format's extension; for stdin, the scratch area.
  --scratch             Write the output to the brain's scratch area
                        (.brain/scratch/): transient, openable in the UI, never
                        committed, and pruned after 7 days or past 1 GB.
  --as <type>           Treat input as markdown or html. Default: from the file
                        extension; markdown for stdin.
  --title <text>        Document title. Default: the frontmatter title, else the
                        file name. A complete HTML document keeps its own.
  --no-running-title    PDF footer shows page numbers only, not the title.
  --width <px>          Layout width, 320-4096. Default 768.
  --allow-host <host>   Let the page load images from this host (repeatable).
                        Off by default: the page resolves no hostname at all.

Designed documents are composed from the shell's components, never from CSS:

  --kind list               The document kinds, and when to use each.
  --kind <kind> --scaffold  Print that kind's skeleton, a complete example to
                            fill in (HTML, or markdown for "note").
  --blocks [name...]        Print the component snippets, all or the named ones.

These three print text and need no input path.

Frontmatter is stripped before rendering — it is metadata, not content. Every
render is checked for what would come out wrong (unknown component classes, an
opener that is not first, images or stylesheets that cannot load, placeholders
left from a skeleton); the findings are printed and listed in \`warnings\`.

PDF and PNG need the optional @schlessera/brain-render-puppeteer package and a
Chrome binary; --format html needs neither and produces the same document.

--json envelope: { input, output, format, bytes, pages, title, allowHosts, warnings }
--kind list --json: { kinds: [{ name, aliases, label, use, opener, blocks, accent, switches, format }] }
"
,
  },
  "schedule --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"  brain schedule add --server ORIGIN [--credential-file FILE] --key KEY --prompt TEXT (--at ISO|--cron EXPR)
      [--time-zone ZONE] [--client-time-zone ZONE] [--end-at ISO] --scope-file FILE
      [--attempt-timeout-ms N] [--max-operations N] [--notify-success] [--approve] [--json]
  brain schedule list --server ORIGIN [--credential-file FILE] [--id ID] [--state STATE] [--limit N] [--cursor CURSOR] [--json]
  brain schedule cancel ID --server ORIGIN [--credential-file FILE] --key KEY [--json]
  brain schedule reconcile ID --server ORIGIN [--credential-file FILE] --key KEY [--json]
  brain schedule due --server ORIGIN [--credential-file FILE] [--limit N] [--cursor CURSOR] [--json]
"
,
  },
  "scratch --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain scratch <clean|prune> — the brain's scratch area (.brain/scratch/)

Transient output (renders, image drafts) lands in the scratch area: inside the
brain so the UI can open it, gitignored so it is never committed, and pruned so
it cannot grow without bound. Retained conversation links pin files; move other files out of it to keep them.

  clean    Remove everything in the scratch area.
  prune    Remove files older than 7 days, then the oldest until it is under
           1 GB. render, image and okf export do this after writing there,
           \`brain maintain\` does it as its last step, and the chat server
           runs it hourly. A
           brain with no chat server has no other periodic pass: schedule
           \`brain maintain\` (cron), or nothing prunes between writes.

prune reads .brain/scratch-pins/pins.json on server-managed brains.
--pins-file <path> reads an explicit snapshot too. Missing, invalid or incomplete
managed pin data suspends deletion (exit 2). Pinned files survive both passes;
over-cap pins are reported, never deleted. clean is an explicit deletion.

Both refuse a \`.brain\` or \`.brain/scratch\` that is a symlink: nothing is
removed anywhere a link points.

--json envelope: { action, removed: [{ path, bytes, reason }], failed: [{ path, reason }], bytes, files, pinned?: { files, bytes }, overageBytes?: number, pinError?: string }
Exits 2 when a file could not be removed (it is listed in failed); the report is printed either way.
"
,
  },
  "search --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain search <query> — hybrid FTS5 + vector search

  --type <type>           Filter by document type
  --tag <tag>             Filter by tag
  --relevance <level>     Filter by relevance (primary|secondary|historical)
  --status <status>       Filter by status (active|archived|draft)
  --mode <mode>           Search mode: fts|vector|hybrid (default: hybrid)
  --rerank <mode>         none|heuristic|jev (default: heuristic; configured
                          provider when reranker.enabled is true and available).
                          jev requires opt-in and orders by relevance judgment;
                          heuristic applies lifecycle factors locally
  --rerank-dry-run        Print the exact rerank request to stderr, send
                          nothing, and return the retrieval ordering
  --include-archived      Include archived documents
  --assets-only           Only return non-markdown assets (images, PDFs)
  --chunks                Add each result's chunks that match the query, best
                          first ({ path, chunk_index, heading, content, score })
  --limit <n>             Max results (default: 20)
  --updated-since <date>  Only documents updated on or after this date (YYYY-MM-DD)
  --updated-before <date> Only documents updated on or before this date
  --deadline-from <date>  Only documents with a deadline on or after this date
  --deadline-to <date>    Only documents with a deadline on or before this date
  --sort <order>          score|updated|deadline (default: score); updated is
                          newest first, deadline is earliest first, undated last
  --upcoming              Same as --deadline-from <today> --sort deadline

--json envelope: { "results": SearchResult[], "warnings": string[] }
"
,
  },
  "setup --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain setup — idempotent first-run wiring

Installs the packaged git hooks (copied into .githooks/ + core.hooksPath set),
syncs skills into per-agent locations, and links the brain CLI into ~/.local/bin.
Safe to re-run.
"
,
  },
  "skills --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain skills <sync|lint>

  sync   Materialize skills into .agents/skills and run per-agent emitters
  lint   Lint discovered skills; discovery warnings (unparseable frontmatter)
         are treated as ERRORS. Exits 1 on any error.
"
,
  },
  "stats --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain stats — corpus statistics and health figures

  --all                   Print every row of every breakdown (default: the top
                          5 by count, then a \`+N more\` remainder line)

Health, printed first: broken-link rate, embedding coverage, stale / orphan /
untagged documents. Its warn levels come from the \`stats\` config block,
defaulting to coverageFloor 0.9, brokenLinkCeiling 0.05, and each health line names
the level it applied. Staleness has no level of its own — it is the taxonomy's
per-type \`staleDays\` (default 180), the window \`brain audit\` already uses, and
the stale line names the windows in force. Orphan means what \`brain audit\`
means, honouring orphanExempt.

Inventory, printed second: documents (by type/status/relevance), tags, links,
chunks, corpus bytes and files on disk (configured excludes apply), brain.db
bytes and row counts, free space on the volume. An \`Embeddings\` row appears
when vectors were counted, and reads \`n/a\` when the brain has a vector table
this host could not read (sqlite-vec did not load).

A figure that cannot be measured is reported as \`n/a\` (\`null\` in --json),
never as 0, and carries no verdict.

Recorded trends compare two non-overlapping seven-day UTC windows ending
today, with at least three valid daily observations each and a recording no
older than 48 hours. Core reports comparison evidence and explicit insufficient,
stale or incomparable states; missing history is never a healthy trend. The
initial warning rules are uncalibrated. See docs/cli.md for the rules.

--json: unaffected by --all — it always carries the full, uncapped breakdowns.

  --record                Also keep today's figures in .stats-history.jsonl at
                          the brain root (one snapshot per UTC day; a second
                          run the same day replaces it). \`brain maintain\`
                          records one on every run.
  --history               Print the recorded snapshots instead, oldest first.
                          --json gives one array per field, ready to chart.
  --since <YYYY-MM-DD>    With --history: only snapshots from that day on.

The history keeps every day for the last 90 days, then one snapshot per week.
It is committed with the brain; \`brain index --force\` never touches it.
"
,
  },
  "storage --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain storage inventory --json
brain storage pack [--dry-run] --json

Collect metadata for BRAIN_ROOT without evaluating brain configuration, opening
a database or reading attachment bytes. Linux descriptor traversal is required.
The finite snapshot includes hierarchy pages (200 entries), observation coverage,
logical/object-union bytes and qualified filesystem capacity. Page cursors belong
to this snapshot only and expire after 60 seconds. A new invocation is a new scan.
Trusted hosts may set BRAIN_STORAGE_GIT_ROOTS to a JSON array of allowed external
Git directories. No request flag accepts or expands those roots.

pack runs the existing loose-object/incremental-pack/ref-packing maintenance.
It preserves refs, reflogs and history. Old and new packs can coexist; temporary
headroom uses a conservative raw-object sizing rule and is not reserved by the
standalone CLI. --dry-run reports the plan without starting maintenance. Exit 2 retains a qualified
refusal/partial report; packing never runs broad gc or expires history.
"
,
  },
  "sync --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain sync [verb] — knowledge-aware brain synchronization

With no verb, runs the whole sync (\`run\`) and prints its report; then, when
an agent runner is configured, hands the agent (/sync skill) what the rules
could not settle: a conflict no strategy resolves, or a file nothing could
classify. Media is never handed on: the report counts the files awaiting
review (\`Media awaiting review: N\`) and hygiene review decides them. With --json it
prints one result instead: the run's envelope and whether an agent was
invoked, and if so which runtime it reported.

  run          The whole sync: stash, assess --fix, commit, pull, resolve,
               conclude, push (re-pulling up to 3 times), post-sync.
               Exit 0 complete, 3 needs-judgment (an open conflict or conflict
               markers in HEAD push nothing), 1 failed
  assess       Classify local changes (SENSITIVE|ARTIFACT|DERIVED|TRACK|MEDIA|LARGE|UNKNOWN;
               MEDIA and LARGE carry their size in bytes)
    --fix      Also ignore ARTIFACT files and unmistakable secrets (.env,
               .env.*, *.key, *.pem) in one .gitignore commit, hold other
               SENSITIVE files back as UNKNOWN, and ask the judge about
               UNKNOWN files when it is on
  group        Group tracked changes by taxonomy domain
  commit       Commit TRACK files, one commit per domain, bumping \`updated\`;
               a file holding conflict markers is left uncommitted (exit 1)
    --plan     Print the commit plan as JSON and change nothing
    --plan-file <path>  Apply an edited plan (messages as given, files checked)
  stash        Drop stash entries the tree already holds; pop a clean autostash
    --dry-run  Report what it would do
  pull         Fetch origin/main and fast-forward or merge, first finishing a
               merge left pending whose only conflicts are the derived caches
  resolve      Merge each conflicted file by its strategy (Jev judges passage
               pairs when TYPESAFE_API_KEY is set); leaves the rest for an agent
  conflicts    Emit BASE/OURS/THEIRS for each conflicted file
  conclude     Once nothing is unmerged, commit a pending merge or squash, or
               unstage what a conflicted stash pop left (its entry stays)
  push         Push to origin/main
  post-sync    Re-sync skills + reindex, commit the derived caches it rewrote,
               then report head parity and any remaining working-tree dirt
"
,
  },
  "tags --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain tags — report tag variants, redundant tags and vocabulary drift (read-only)

Reads every document's frontmatter tags and reports:
  variant groups     tags that normalize to one key (-/_ stripped, English
                     singular, small spelling distance), with a proposed canonical
  redundant tags     a tag equal to the document's type or a directory of its path
  alias hits         a tag that is a key of taxonomy.tags.aliases
  out of vocabulary  tags outside taxonomy.tags.vocabulary, when one is set

Nothing is rewritten. Configure the vocabulary under taxonomy.tags in brain.config.

--json envelope: { "tags", "documents", "variantGroups", "redundant", "aliasHits", "outOfVocabulary" }

brain tags --apply — migrate frontmatter tags to their canonical forms

Applies every taxonomy.tags.aliases entry, and every variant group whose
proposed canonical is in the vocabulary. Only the tags entries of each file
change: every other byte, the updated date included, stays as it was. The
touched files are reindexed and their mtimes accepted, so they do not show up
as silently modified.

  --groups                Apply every variant group, in the vocabulary or not
  --redundant             Also remove tags that repeat the document's type or directory
  --only <old>            Only change this one old tag
  --dry-run               Report what would change and write nothing

A file edited while the command runs is skipped ("changed during apply"). An
index that cannot be opened stops the run before any file is rewritten. A
file that cannot be read or written is reported with a reason starting
"failed:", the files already rewritten are still indexed and accepted, and
the command exits 2.

--json envelope: { "files": [{ "path", "from", "to" }], "skipped": [{ "path", "reason" }], "warnings": string[] }
"
,
  },
  "validate --help": {
    "code": 0,
    "stdout": 
"brain validate — config check, then frontmatter + wiki-link + index-drift validation.
"
,
  },
}
`;
